<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><title>QR Code Guide: What to Encode and How to Use It | SmartToolz</title><meta name="description" content="Understand QR codes, what information they can contain and practical tips for making a QR code easy to scan."><link rel="canonical" href="https://smarttoolz.in/blog/qr-code-guide-what-to-encode-and-how-to-use-it/"><?php require dirname(__DIR__) . '/head.php'; ?></head><body><?php require dirname(__DIR__) . '/header.php'; ?><main class="container py-5" style="max-width:900px"><article><span class="eyebrow">GENERATOR GUIDE</span><h1 class="display-5 fw-bold mt-3">QR Code Guide: What to Encode and How to Use It</h1><p class="lead text-muted">A QR code is a compact way to share information through a camera scan. The best results come from simple content, clear contrast and enough physical size for the intended distance.</p><h2>What can a QR code contain?</h2><p>Common uses include website URLs, plain text, contact details, Wi-Fi information and other structured strings supported by the scanning app. Decide what the recipient should do after scanning before creating the code.</p><h2>Keep the destination useful</h2><p>If the QR code points to a website, make sure the destination works on phones and does not require unnecessary steps. For printed material, avoid changing the destination without testing the final printed code.</p><h2>Test before publishing</h2><p>Scan the generated code with more than one phone when possible. Test it at the approximate distance and lighting where people will use it. A code that scans perfectly on a large monitor may be harder to scan when printed very small.</p><h2>For clean results</h2><ul><li>Leave a clear margin around the QR pattern.</li><li>Use strong contrast between the code and its background.</li><li>Avoid covering the pattern with text or decoration.</li><li>Test the final image or print before sharing it.</li></ul><p><a href="/tools/">Create a QR code with SmartToolz →</a></p></article></main><?php require dirname(__DIR__) . '/footer.php'; ?></body></html>
